---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { SITE, LEGAL, CLAIMS } from '../lib/site.js';
import { LICENCE_STATES } from '../lib/licensing.js';
import { guides } from '../lib/guides.js';
import attribution from '../data/overture-attribution.json';

// The 2026-10-21 audit's E-E-A-T finding was that claims like "$260 national
// average" carry no citation and no stated method, on content that is
// YMYL-adjacent. Chris's answer, 2026-10-31, was the right one: rather than
// inventing a credential, say where the numbers come from and let a reader
// check the reasoning.
//
// So this page states the method, including the parts that are weaker than a
// reader might assume. A methodology page that only lists strengths is
// marketing. The sections on what the figures are NOT, and on the 47 states
// with no register to check, are the ones that make the rest believable.
//
// DELIBERATELY NOT CITED: specific DASMA technical data sheets. DASMA is real
// and publishes numbered TDS documents, but the ones covering spring cycle
// life were not read before writing this, and citing a document by number to
// borrow its authority is exactly the failure this page exists to correct.
// If those sheets are read later, cite them then.
const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'How we price these jobs', url: '/how-we-price/' },
];
const states = Object.values(LICENCE_STATES);
const dated = guides
  .map((g) => g.dateModified || g.datePublished)
  .filter(Boolean)
  .sort();
const oldest = dated[0];
const newest = dated[dated.length - 1];
---
<BaseLayout
  title="How We Work Out These Prices"
  description="Where the cost figures on this site come from, how the ranges are set, what they are not, and how to check them against a quote you have been given."
  canonical="/how-we-price/"
>
  <div class="wrap-narrow">
    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />

    <article style="margin-top:1rem;">
      <h1 style="margin:1rem 0 .5rem;">How we work out these prices</h1>
      <p class="muted" style="margin:0 0 2rem;font-size:.85rem;">
        Last updated: {LEGAL.updated.howWePrice}
      </p>

      <div class="answer-box">
        <p>
          Every cost on this site is a <strong>national range for 2026, researched by us</strong>.
          It is not taken from a published price index, a trade body's figures, or any
          licensed dataset — there is no such source for US garage door work that we could
          republish. Knowing that is the point of this page: you should be able to see how
          firm a number is before you lean on it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="prose">
        <h2>What the figures are</h2>
        <p>
          Ranges, not quotes. A range is honest about something a single number hides: the
          same job costs differently in Houston and in Santa Monica, on a single door and a
          double, at 9am on a Tuesday and at 9pm on a Sunday. Where one of those factors
          moves the price predictably, the guide says so next to the number rather than
          averaging it away.
        </p>
        <p>
          They are national. We do not publish a metro-level price, because we do not have
          metro-level data we could stand behind, and inventing one city's figure by
          nudging a national average would be a worse answer dressed as a better one.
        </p>

        <h2>Where a number comes from</h2>
        <p>
          Three different things sit behind the figures on this site, and they are not
          equally strong. It is worth knowing which you are reading.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Arithmetic.</strong> Some figures are not estimates at all. A spring rated
          at 10,000 cycles, on a door opened four times a day, lasts 10,000 ÷ 4 ÷ 365 ≈ 6.8
          years — so the guides say roughly seven, and about three and a half at eight
          openings a day. There is no judgement in that, and a build check now fails if any
          page states a cycle life that does not match the division.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Component cost plus labor.</strong> Where a part has a knowable price, the
          range is built from it. A torsion spring is a $40–$90 component; the rest of a
          $180–$350 replacement is skilled, genuinely hazardous labor and the service call. We
          say which half is which, because that is the split that tells you whether a quote
          is high on parts, high on hours, or fair.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Observed market rates.</strong> The rest is our own research into what the
          work is actually quoted at across the US in 2026. This is the softest input, and
          the honest description of it is that it is ours — not a citation we can hand you.
          Where we are least certain, the range is wider rather than falsely precise.
        </p>

        <h2>What these figures are not</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Not a quote.</strong> Nobody on this site has seen your door. A real quote can sit outside our range for good reasons.</li>
          <li><strong>Not a price we negotiated.</strong> We have no commercial arrangement with any company listed, and no contractor sees these pages before they publish.</li>
          <li><strong>Not sourced from the listings.</strong> The figures are written in the cost guides and read from there by every city and business page. They are not derived from the businesses in the directory.</li>
          <li><strong>Not a judgement on any company.</strong> A business quoting above our range is not overcharging. It may be more experienced, more insured, or faster.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>How they are kept consistent</h2>
        <p>
          A figure lives in exactly one place — the guide that owns it — and every other page
          reads it from there rather than restating it. That is enforced rather than
          remembered: the build fails if the same job carries two different ranges anywhere
          on the site, and it fails if a stated cycle life disagrees with the arithmetic. A
          site that argues with itself about a price has told you its numbers are decorative.
        </p>
        <p>
          Every guide carries a visible last-updated date. The current set was last touched
          between {oldest} and {newest}. When a figure changes, everywhere quoting it changes
          in the same edit.
        </p>

        <h2>What we check, and what we cannot</h2>
        <p>
          {CLAIMS.licenceMeaning} In {states.map((s) => s.state).join(', ')} that check is
          possible because each of those states publishes a contractor register. California
          and Florida license garage door work as its own classification; Arizona licenses
          the contractor rather than the trade. In the other 47 there is no register to
          check, so a missing badge there means nothing was checkable — not that something
          failed.
        </p>
        <p>
          Where a listing shows what a company offers, that is read from the company's own
          website. {CLAIMS.siteFactsMeaning}
        </p>
        <p>
          The business records themselves come from {attribution.source}, under the
          {' '}{attribution.license}. {CLAIMS.listingMeaning}
        </p>

        <h2>Who writes this</h2>
        <p>
          {SITE.name} is written and edited by Chris Cooper, who researches and publishes
          these figures. He is not a garage door technician, and this site does not inspect
          anyone's work. That is exactly why the pages lean on things you can check for
          yourself — a license number against a state register, a price split into parts and
          labor, a cycle rating in writing — rather than on an opinion you would have to
          take on trust.
        </p>
        <p>
          It is also why there are no repair instructions for springs or cables anywhere on
          this site, at any search volume. A partial instruction for a job that stores enough
          energy to break bones is more dangerous than no instruction at all.
        </p>

        <h2>If a figure looks wrong</h2>
        <p>
          Tell us and we will check it:
          {' '}<a href={`mailto:${LEGAL.contactEmail}`}>{LEGAL.contactEmail}</a>. A quote you
          have been given that sits well outside one of our ranges is useful evidence, and
          corrections are published rather than quietly applied. The
          {' '}<a href="/editorial-guidelines/">editorial guidelines</a> cover how the rest of
          the directory is built.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
